Acute Stroke
Acute Urgent Care
Unilateral facial droop, Hemiparesis, Aphasia/Dysarthria

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-014
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of facial drooping, inability to speak clearly, and right-sided weakness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult son
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a sudden onset of focal neurological deficits including facial droop and severe speech impairment within the last 45 minutes. An immediate face-to-face assessment was critical to confirm a stroke code and expedite emergency transport, as intravenous thrombolysis or endovascular thrombectomy is strictly time-dependent and cannot be delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Son reports] He suddenly dropped his coffee cup, the right side of his face sagged, and now he can't get any words out that make sense.
History of Present Illness: The patient experienced a sudden onset of focal neurological deficits 45 minutes prior to the assessment. The son noted an immediate unilateral facial droop on the right side and a sudden lack of coordination resulting in the patient dropping a cup. The patient exhibits severe expressive aphasia, attempting to speak but producing unintelligible sounds, accompanied by marked dysarthria. A profound unilateral right arm and leg weakness (hemiparesis) has developed. The patient remains conscious but appears highly frustrated by the inability to communicate.
Functional Status: Non-ambulant due to sudden right-sided paralysis; seated in a chair.
Social/Safety Concerns: Total loss of independence; high fall risk due to dense hemiplegia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 175/95 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient is alert but distressed, making frustrated gestures with the left arm while the right arm hangs flaccid.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination confirms a profound right-sided dense hemiparesis (power 0/5 in right upper limb, 1/5 in right lower limb). There is a clear right-sided lower facial droop sparing the forehead (upper motor neuron lesion). Severe expressive aphasia is present; the patient follows simple one-step commands (receptive function partially intact) but cannot name objects or repeat phrases. Leftward gaze deviation is noted. Cardiovascular exam reveals an irregularly irregular pulse. Expected investigations include an acute MRI brain showing restricted diffusion or a non-contrast CT head demonstrating an acute infarction or ruling out haemorrhage.
Environmental Observations: Cleared the immediate area of trip hazards to facilitate stretcher access.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has a known history of atrial fibrillation but ceased novel oral anticoagulants (NOACs) 3 months ago due to cost.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Stroke - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Bell's Palsy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the facial droop sparing the forehead (indicating a central lesion), and the presence of severe arm/leg weakness and aphasia, which are completely absent in a peripheral nerve palsy.

Hypoglycaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to a normal capillary blood glucose measurement (5.8 mmol/L) taken on arrival, eliminating low blood sugar as a stroke mimic.

Complex Partial Seizure (Todd's Paresis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of witnessed seizure activity, sudden flaccid onset rather than post-ictal confusion, and classic vascular territory signs (aphasia with right hemiparesis).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic diagnostic tetrad for a major acute ischemic stroke involving the left middle cerebral artery: sudden unilateral facial droop, right-sided hemiparesis, severe aphasia, and sudden onset. The objective finding of an irregularly irregular pulse suggests a cardioembolic etiology. Urgent, immediate after-hours assessment was mandatory to identify the exact "last known well" time and rule out stroke mimics like hypoglycemia. Delaying evaluation would have pushed the patient beyond the strict 4.5-hour window for systemic thrombolysis and the 24-hour window for endovascular clot retrieval, guaranteeing permanent, devastating neurological disability.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Measured capillary blood glucose (5.8 mmol/L). Patient kept Nil By Mouth (NBM) due to high aspiration risk from dysphagia. Activated a pre-hospital Stroke Code by calling 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport directly to a comprehensive stroke center.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your blood thinners every day exactly as prescribed, you prevent the blood from pooling in your heart and forming the exact type of clots that break off and cause these devastating brain blockages.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency transport to a Stroke Center.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics and hospital stroke team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The son is instructed to keep the patient seated and strictly nil by mouth. If the patient's level of consciousness declines, position them in the recovery position on their non-paralyzed (left) side to protect the airway.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: You are suffering a major stroke. The hospital needs to perform an urgent brain scan to see if it is caused by a clot or a bleed.
Clinical Course: If a clot is confirmed and treated rapidly with clot-busting drugs or physical removal, some or all of the lost movement and speech may be recovered.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not try to eat or drink anything, not even a sip of water, because the muscles in your throat are weak and the fluid will go into your lungs. We are getting you to the hospital right now for emergency treatment.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain is currently not getting enough blood because of a blockage. We are transferring you immediately to a specialized team that can dissolve or pull that blockage out to save your brain tissue.
Actionable Steps: Try to remain as calm as possible. Let your son do all the communicating with the paramedics. Keep your paralyzed arm resting securely on your lap so it does not get injured during the transfer.

